WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.167 --> 00:00:02.501
-Our next guests
are comedians and writers

00:00:02.501 --> 00:00:04.704
whom you know from their work
on "Saturday Night Live,"

00:00:04.704 --> 00:00:07.273
which airs this Saturday
with host Timothee Chalamet

00:00:07.273 --> 00:00:09.642
and musical guests Boygenius.
They wrote and starred

00:00:09.642 --> 00:00:12.645
in "Please Don't Destroy: The
Treasure of Foggy Mountain,"

00:00:12.645 --> 00:00:14.948
which is streaming
on Peacock November 17th.

00:00:14.948 --> 00:00:15.982
Let's take a look.

00:00:15.982 --> 00:00:18.117
[ Organ playing ]

00:00:18.117 --> 00:00:22.222
-Whoo! Yay, baby! You got this.
You're so good.

00:00:22.222 --> 00:00:23.956
-Go, Mart!
-Thank you guys

00:00:23.956 --> 00:00:25.125
for coming, by the way.

00:00:25.125 --> 00:00:26.626
I know it means a lot to him
that you're here.

00:00:26.626 --> 00:00:28.694
-Amy, of course.

00:00:28.694 --> 00:00:30.329
What the [bleep] is her deal,
man?

00:00:30.329 --> 00:00:32.198
-Shut up.
She's right next to us.

00:00:32.198 --> 00:00:34.733
-What is her problem?
-She's fine, man. She's --

00:00:34.733 --> 00:00:36.502
She's honestly really nice.
It's just...

00:00:36.502 --> 00:00:37.570
I don't know why
Martin is so --

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:39.706
-Afraid?
He's petrified right now.

00:00:39.706 --> 00:00:41.274
-It looks like
he's being held at gunpoint.

00:00:41.274 --> 00:00:43.709
-I know, and what the [bleep] is
going on with that suit?

00:00:43.709 --> 00:00:45.278
-I don't know, man.
-It's huge, dude.

00:00:45.278 --> 00:00:46.846
He looks like the [bleep] sun.

00:00:46.846 --> 00:00:48.915
-He looks like a pimp
in a high school play.

00:00:48.915 --> 00:00:52.118
-I baptize thee in the name
of the Father,

00:00:52.118 --> 00:00:55.588
the Son and the Holy Ghost.

00:00:55.588 --> 00:00:58.625
You are now baptized.
-Whoo!

00:00:58.625 --> 00:01:02.328
That was so good.
[ Applause ]

00:01:02.328 --> 00:01:04.797
-Please welcome to the show
from the comedy group

00:01:04.797 --> 00:01:06.932
Please Don't Destroy,
Martin Herlihy,

00:01:06.932 --> 00:01:08.634
John Higgins, and Ben Marshall.

00:01:08.634 --> 00:01:10.002
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:10.002 --> 00:01:29.989 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:29.989 --> 00:01:32.024
Welcome, gentlemen.
-Hi!

00:01:32.024 --> 00:01:34.727
-I'm so happy to have you
and such nice timing.

00:01:34.727 --> 00:01:36.829
I also though
want to compliment you.

00:01:36.829 --> 00:01:38.398
You were going to come
no matter what.

00:01:38.398 --> 00:01:40.066
We just weren't going to talk
about the movie.

00:01:40.066 --> 00:01:42.668
And as someone with a show,
I appreciated

00:01:42.668 --> 00:01:44.104
that you were not
going to leave us scrambling.

00:01:44.104 --> 00:01:45.137
So thank you.
-We love Seth Meyers.

00:01:45.137 --> 00:01:46.272
-Oh, that's very kind.

00:01:46.272 --> 00:01:47.740
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:50.676
And I been a huge fan of yours
from even before "SNL."

00:01:50.676 --> 00:01:54.580
We were actually talking about
working on a project together.

00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:55.781
I mean, you guys
are still young,

00:01:55.781 --> 00:01:57.217
but I felt like you
were even younger then.

00:01:57.217 --> 00:01:59.618
That's how time flies.
-Yeah, I remember

00:01:59.618 --> 00:02:02.055
we were at dinner with you
when we got an offer

00:02:02.055 --> 00:02:04.056
to play our first college.
-Yeah.

00:02:04.056 --> 00:02:06.425
-At Iowa State University.
-Iowa State. Yeah.

00:02:06.425 --> 00:02:08.928
-And we were like, "This is
going to change our lives."

00:02:08.928 --> 00:02:12.298
-It was the best
because you guys were so happy.

00:02:12.298 --> 00:02:13.700
I remember,
I think John got the e-mail

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:15.134
and we were in the middle
of dinner and you're like,

00:02:15.134 --> 00:02:17.704
"Guys, drop everything."
-"Dude."

00:02:17.704 --> 00:02:20.740
-"We're going to Iowa!"
[ Laughter ]

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:22.441
-At dinner with Seth Meyers.
It's like,

00:02:22.441 --> 00:02:26.445
"Shut up, shut up. Iowa State."

00:02:26.445 --> 00:02:27.714
-In their main arena?

00:02:27.714 --> 00:02:30.516
No, it's like
a smaller theater in the...

00:02:30.516 --> 00:02:33.119
-It's going to be
about half full.

00:02:33.119 --> 00:02:34.754
-But I remember -- I don't know
if you guys remember.

00:02:34.754 --> 00:02:37.490
I was like, "Oh, try
to not forget. This is awesome."

00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:39.192
Like as happy
as you are today.

00:02:39.192 --> 00:02:41.627
It's a hard thing to hold on to.

00:02:41.627 --> 00:02:42.795
But you guys were --
-Much happier then.

00:02:42.795 --> 00:02:44.196
-Yeah. Much happier then, right.

00:02:44.196 --> 00:02:46.665
Because now you're on "SNL"
and you have to fail every week.

00:02:46.665 --> 00:02:47.900
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:49.835
-Or the potential of failure
is very real.

00:02:49.835 --> 00:02:52.438
This is the third season
for you guys.

00:02:52.438 --> 00:02:55.008
Certainly from watching
on the show,

00:02:55.008 --> 00:02:57.810
you guys have an incredible
success rate with your shorts.

00:02:57.810 --> 00:03:00.112
But does it feel
more at home now?

00:03:00.112 --> 00:03:01.013
-Maybe.
-Yeah.

00:03:01.013 --> 00:03:02.949
-Oh.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:02.949 --> 00:03:04.316
We do.
-Were you going to say no?

00:03:04.316 --> 00:03:07.853
-I was going to say
maybe next year but I...

00:03:07.853 --> 00:03:10.890
-I feel more at home in that,
like, this is our home

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:13.158
because we, like, live here now.
-Yes, yes.

00:03:13.158 --> 00:03:17.430
-We used to stay here until
like 6 a.m. two nights ago.

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:19.465
-In the sense
that home is where you sleep.

00:03:19.465 --> 00:03:21.967
-Yeah, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:21.967 --> 00:03:25.004
Who among you, like,
for example, Bad Bunny,

00:03:25.004 --> 00:03:26.538
you did
a wonderful sketch with him

00:03:26.538 --> 00:03:29.008
where he was dressed like Shrek

00:03:29.008 --> 00:03:31.477
and he was pitching you guys
a Shrek sketch.

00:03:31.477 --> 00:03:34.414
So I'm going to guess
that one came up late at night.

00:03:34.414 --> 00:03:35.814
[ Laughter ]

00:03:35.814 --> 00:03:39.885
And like, who among you has
the first seed of this idea?

00:03:39.885 --> 00:03:41.687
-That was Marty.
-Marty had that.

00:03:41.687 --> 00:03:43.823
-And how does it come
to you, Martin?

00:03:43.823 --> 00:03:47.627
-I think it was
a panic sort of situation.

00:03:47.627 --> 00:03:48.961
-Yeah.
-That one.

00:03:48.961 --> 00:03:50.430
But that one, we wrote it.

00:03:50.430 --> 00:03:53.466
We like maybe started it
at like midnight or 1:00,

00:03:53.466 --> 00:03:56.369
and then we added some stuff
throughout the course

00:03:56.369 --> 00:03:58.137
of the week,
and then the final edit

00:03:58.137 --> 00:04:00.373
is exactly what we wrote
at midnight.

00:04:00.373 --> 00:04:02.542
-Oh, see, that happened a lot.

00:04:02.542 --> 00:04:04.643
Sometimes you would write
something,

00:04:04.643 --> 00:04:07.179
it would get picked
and then you overwrite it.

00:04:07.179 --> 00:04:08.547
-Yes.
-And then you realize,

00:04:08.547 --> 00:04:11.216
oh, weirdly, the crazy idea
we had was the best idea.

00:04:11.216 --> 00:04:13.386
I will say to your credit,
Martin, that is a funny idea.

00:04:13.386 --> 00:04:14.987
[ Laughter ]

00:04:14.987 --> 00:04:16.355
Were you worried
when you came up with it?

00:04:16.355 --> 00:04:19.025
"Oh, I bet a lot of other people
are writing him as Shrek."

00:04:19.025 --> 00:04:20.459
[ Laughter ]

00:04:20.459 --> 00:04:22.528
-It's so top of mind
right now.

00:04:22.528 --> 00:04:24.196
-It is.
When you see Bad Bunny,

00:04:24.196 --> 00:04:26.498
you're like, what if?

00:04:26.498 --> 00:04:29.702
Alright, so the movie,
you guys want to set up

00:04:29.702 --> 00:04:31.237
the premise
of the film real quick?

00:04:31.237 --> 00:04:34.006
-Yes.
The movie, it's crazy at "SNL"

00:04:34.006 --> 00:04:36.876
because we just play like Ben,
John and Martin,

00:04:36.876 --> 00:04:38.944
these like friends
who work together.

00:04:38.944 --> 00:04:41.713
So in this movie, we got to play
Ben, John and Martin,

00:04:41.713 --> 00:04:43.249
who work together.
-Yeah.

00:04:43.249 --> 00:04:44.417
[ Laughter ]

00:04:44.417 --> 00:04:46.252
For me, it was thrilling
to see the departure.

00:04:46.252 --> 00:04:47.586
-Yeah, it's nice.

00:04:47.586 --> 00:04:50.389
-We have so much range.
-Yeah.

00:04:50.389 --> 00:04:53.159
-Did you ever consider
giving yourself different names?

00:04:53.159 --> 00:04:54.493
-No.
-Yeah.

00:04:54.493 --> 00:04:56.062
-We thought about it
for a second,

00:04:56.062 --> 00:04:59.499
but I couldn't imagine being
like, "Steve, don't do that."

00:04:59.499 --> 00:05:00.566
[ Laughter ]

00:05:00.566 --> 00:05:02.735
-"Shut up, Eric!"
-"Eric!"

00:05:02.735 --> 00:05:04.002
-We both went with Eric.
-Yeah.

00:05:04.002 --> 00:05:06.505
-We did.
Yeah, but the movie,

00:05:06.505 --> 00:05:09.375
it's about three losers
named Ben, Martin, and John.

00:05:09.375 --> 00:05:10.742
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:05:10.742 --> 00:05:14.513
-That's not funny.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:14.513 --> 00:05:18.451
Who, like, are
feeling stuck in their lives.

00:05:18.451 --> 00:05:21.487
And they think the way to change
that is to go out

00:05:21.487 --> 00:05:24.691
and find treasure
on their local mountain.

00:05:24.691 --> 00:05:26.959
Kind of like "Goonies"
for 27-year-olds.

00:05:26.959 --> 00:05:28.293
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:28.293 --> 00:05:30.663
It has a very nice --
I mean this in a genuine way.

00:05:30.663 --> 00:05:33.098
It has a very good "Goonies"
vibe.

00:05:33.098 --> 00:05:34.633
-Thank you.
-Yeah.

00:05:34.633 --> 00:05:37.203
-You told us that you
watched it on your phone, right?

00:05:37.203 --> 00:05:39.505
-I watched your movie
on my phone, yes.

00:05:39.505 --> 00:05:42.475
And the last time I watched
"Goonies" was on an airplane.

00:05:42.475 --> 00:05:44.143
[ Laughter ]

00:05:44.143 --> 00:05:48.147
But you had some very good
casting, Ben, for your father.

00:05:48.147 --> 00:05:51.584
And were you excited when this
person said they would do this?

00:05:51.584 --> 00:05:53.085
-I couldn't believe it.

00:05:53.085 --> 00:05:56.856
-I'm guessing it was like
your first thought was like,

00:05:56.856 --> 00:05:58.224
"What if this person
would say yes?"

00:05:58.224 --> 00:06:01.961
-Yeah, no, he was about 15th
on the list, and...

00:06:01.961 --> 00:06:04.196
Yeah. No, it was amazing.
I mean, do you want to show?

00:06:04.196 --> 00:06:06.398
-It also kind of makes sense.

00:06:06.398 --> 00:06:08.934
I mean, if Conan O'Brien's going
to play any of your dads...

00:06:08.934 --> 00:06:12.571
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:12.571 --> 00:06:16.142
-Yeah. Yeah.
-He's very good.

00:06:16.142 --> 00:06:17.542
-Yeah.
-He's so good.

00:06:17.542 --> 00:06:21.246
And he's weirdly never played
a character in a movie

00:06:21.246 --> 00:06:23.048
that is not Conan O'Brien.
-Right.

00:06:23.048 --> 00:06:25.317
-So it was like
the first time he got to act.

00:06:25.317 --> 00:06:27.553
-Hopefully you guys
weren't judging that

00:06:27.553 --> 00:06:29.455
as you're still your own names.

00:06:29.455 --> 00:06:30.856
[ Laughter ]

00:06:30.856 --> 00:06:33.325
-"Oh, you've never
played a character?

00:06:33.325 --> 00:06:35.594
What a loser. God."

00:06:35.594 --> 00:06:39.098
-So, I mean, it must have been
so cool when he said yes.

00:06:39.098 --> 00:06:41.600
And you guys get to sort of
break him as an actor.

00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:42.901
I feel like that's
really thrilling.

00:06:42.901 --> 00:06:45.571
-Yeah, it was fun to teach him
how show business works.

00:06:45.571 --> 00:06:48.207
-Totally.
-No, it was amazing.

00:06:48.207 --> 00:06:51.676
I mean, he's such a legend.
We've all grown up on his stuff.

00:06:51.676 --> 00:06:53.545
So it was really cool.

00:06:53.545 --> 00:06:55.347
-The clip was
a very good example

00:06:55.347 --> 00:06:56.915
of my favorite thing
in this movie

00:06:56.915 --> 00:06:58.584
is how lovingly you

00:06:58.584 --> 00:07:01.420
all just make fun
of each other constantly.

00:07:01.420 --> 00:07:02.989
This is a very lovely
through line

00:07:02.989 --> 00:07:04.624
in a lot of your shorts as well.

00:07:04.624 --> 00:07:06.725
And I think it works
because we can tell you guys

00:07:06.725 --> 00:07:09.328
like each other.
When you write these jokes

00:07:09.328 --> 00:07:12.965
where you're just like laying
into your closest friends,

00:07:12.965 --> 00:07:16.001
is it ever too close to the bone
or is it always just joy?

00:07:16.001 --> 00:07:17.436
-It's...

00:07:17.436 --> 00:07:19.205
Yeah, sometimes it hurts. Um...

00:07:19.205 --> 00:07:21.841
[ Laughter ]

00:07:21.841 --> 00:07:25.344
What about something about
how your head's really big?

00:07:25.344 --> 00:07:28.080
Maybe.

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:30.983
-We had something in one video
where we called Martin

00:07:30.983 --> 00:07:33.685
like a melted
Harry Potter action figure.

00:07:33.685 --> 00:07:34.854
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:07:34.854 --> 00:07:38.023
-And that one
bordered on too mean.

00:07:38.023 --> 00:07:40.192
-Just being like...
[ Laughs awkwardly ]

00:07:40.192 --> 00:07:41.693
-I think most of the time,

00:07:41.693 --> 00:07:45.731
the relief that we have thought
of a joke surmounts anything.

00:07:45.731 --> 00:07:47.399
-Oh, right.

00:07:47.399 --> 00:07:49.267
So you're like, you don't even
care that it's at your expense.

00:07:49.267 --> 00:07:50.803
You're like, "If this is
a laugh, we will take it."

00:07:50.803 --> 00:07:53.272
-It works. Put it in. It's done.
-Yeah.

00:07:53.272 --> 00:07:55.975
"We'll talk about it
in 15 years in therapy.

00:07:55.975 --> 00:07:57.676
We do not have to deal
with this now."

00:07:57.676 --> 00:08:01.080
You guys also,
I'm just wondering,

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:02.915
like when you're on set
and obviously, you know,

00:08:02.915 --> 00:08:04.616
Judd was probably on set
a fair amount of time.

00:08:04.616 --> 00:08:05.885
You got Conan on set.

00:08:05.885 --> 00:08:07.419
And it's this movie
you guys wrote.

00:08:07.419 --> 00:08:11.357
Was it surreal, like,
as you were shooting this thing?

00:08:11.357 --> 00:08:15.795
-Every day was --
Every day was a dream.

00:08:15.795 --> 00:08:18.263
I couldn't believe -- We grew up
on Judd's movies.

00:08:18.263 --> 00:08:21.934
And to be able to do one
with him was like mind-blowing.

00:08:21.934 --> 00:08:24.169
-Also, like 90% of our videos,

00:08:24.169 --> 00:08:27.239
we shoot in like a 10x10 office.
-Yeah.

00:08:27.239 --> 00:08:30.175
-And so we were like,
there's a scene in the movie

00:08:30.175 --> 00:08:34.346
where I'm being flown up
on a crane in a wing suit,

00:08:34.346 --> 00:08:35.715
like on a mountain,

00:08:35.715 --> 00:08:38.050
and it's just like the scale
was unbelievable.

00:08:38.050 --> 00:08:40.586
-It was crazy.
And it was 102 degrees,

00:08:40.586 --> 00:08:44.022
like every day. We were in
North Carolina and it was --

00:08:44.022 --> 00:08:47.259
Yeah, it was wild.
Yeah, it was really hard.

00:08:47.259 --> 00:08:49.394
-They say at 102, you want to be
in that wing suit.

00:08:49.394 --> 00:08:50.663
-Yeah.
-Yeah, definitely.

00:08:50.663 --> 00:08:54.533
-They had umbrellas
because the sun was too intense.

00:08:54.533 --> 00:08:56.135
-I had a guy come up to me
and said,

00:08:56.135 --> 00:08:58.137
"The science says
if you take your shoes off,

00:08:58.137 --> 00:09:02.741
you'll cool down faster."
Like, it was like a wax suit

00:09:02.741 --> 00:09:05.211
that was like trapping
all the heat in.

00:09:05.211 --> 00:09:07.379
I was being raised
closer to the sun.

00:09:07.379 --> 00:09:10.582
Guys were like pouring
water bottles inside of me.

00:09:10.582 --> 00:09:12.684
I was like, "Let's do
another one. Let's go again.

00:09:12.684 --> 00:09:14.520
Ah!"

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:17.222
-Whenever a guy is
like, "The science says"

00:09:17.222 --> 00:09:19.725
and it's like shoe-related,
it might not be a scientist.

00:09:19.725 --> 00:09:21.026
[ Laughter ]

00:09:21.026 --> 00:09:23.462
Yeah. You guys, you did
something very cool today,

00:09:23.462 --> 00:09:24.763
which I want
everybody here to know.

00:09:24.763 --> 00:09:27.600
You put the first four minutes
of the movie on YouTube,

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:30.235
so right now you can
go check it out.

00:09:30.235 --> 00:09:32.170
It's very funny from the second
the movie starts.

00:09:32.170 --> 00:09:34.106
So I hope everybody will go
do that and check that out.

00:09:34.106 --> 00:09:35.874
-Thank you.
-Thank you so much.

00:09:35.874 --> 00:09:37.877
-Just one long YouTube
video basically.

00:09:37.877 --> 00:09:39.845
-Yeah.
-That's what movies are.

00:09:39.845 --> 00:09:42.681
-And then how are -- my last
thing before I let you guys go.

00:09:42.681 --> 00:09:43.883
How were you feeling
about this week?

00:09:43.883 --> 00:09:45.017
Are you excited
about "SNL" as well?

00:09:45.017 --> 00:09:46.852
It must be a trippy week
to be promoting a movie,

00:09:46.852 --> 00:09:48.854
and also to know
that Lorne doesn't care

00:09:48.854 --> 00:09:50.556
and he wants you
to have a good Saturday.

00:09:50.556 --> 00:09:52.657
[ Laughter ]

00:09:52.657 --> 00:09:53.859
Lorne right now
is probably like,

00:09:53.859 --> 00:09:55.795
"Oh, are they promoting
their movie?"

00:09:55.795 --> 00:09:59.098
[ Laughter ]

00:09:59.098 --> 00:10:02.201
-That is the vibe.
-That's the vibe. Yeah, yeah.

00:10:02.201 --> 00:10:03.702
Guess what.
You're not imagining it.

00:10:03.702 --> 00:10:05.671
-No.
-No.

00:10:05.671 --> 00:10:07.439
-No, we did "The Tonight Show"
on Monday

00:10:07.439 --> 00:10:09.174
and he was like,
"You're late for pitch."

00:10:09.174 --> 00:10:10.609
-Yeah, yeah.
-Yeah.

00:10:10.609 --> 00:10:11.777
-That's what he does.
That's how he gets the best work

00:10:11.777 --> 00:10:12.945
out of you guys.
And he certainly has.

